<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog
        xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
        xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
        xsi:schemaLocation="
          http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
          https://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.23.xsd">

    <!--
      "Odaberi lika" — a drawn character instead of an uploaded photo.

      Most people never upload an avatar, so most profiles render as a grey
      circle with initials. The sixteen faces live in the FRONTEND
      (frontend/src/components/avatars/avatarArt.ts); all the backend ever
      stores is WHICH one, as its id string.

      Why a plain varchar and not an FK to a lookup table: the drawings are
      code, not data. There is nothing to join to, nothing an organiser can
      add, and a lookup table would mean two places to keep in step instead of
      one. The id is validated against AvatarPresetService.PRESETS on the way
      in, so the column can only hold something the renderer knows.

      varchar(32) matches the DTO's @Size. The longest id today is 8
      characters; this is a cap on nonsense, not a real limit.

      Nullable, because null is a real state and means two different things
      that the app does not need to tell apart: "never had one assigned" and
      "explicitly cleared". Both render as initials.

      APPEND-ONLY, like the frontend list: renaming an id orphans every profile
      that picked it, and the row would then render as initials again with no
      way to notice.
    -->

    <changeSet id="2026-09-10-avatar-preset" author="mrodek">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <columnExists tableName="user_profiles" columnName="avatar_preset"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>

        <addColumn tableName="user_profiles">
            <column name="avatar_preset" type="varchar(32)"/>
        </addColumn>

        <rollback>
            <dropColumn tableName="user_profiles" columnName="avatar_preset"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <!--
      Backfill: give every existing profile a face, the same one the Java side
      would have picked.

      The assignment is a deterministic function of the Firebase UID — see
      AvatarPresetService.defaultFor for why it is not random. The two
      implementations have to agree exactly, which is the whole reason the hash
      is MD5 and not Java's String.hashCode(): hashCode is not reproducible in
      SQL, MD5 is available identically in both.

        SQL   : (('x' || substr(md5(uid),1,7))::bit(28)::bigint % 16)
        Java  : Long.parseLong(md5Hex(uid).substring(0, 7), 16) % 16

      28 bits rather than 32 on purpose: Postgres casts bit(32) to a SIGNED
      32-bit integer, so a hash whose top bit is set comes out negative and the
      modulo would land on a negative index. 28 bits is always non-negative.
      AvatarPresetServiceTest pins the two against each other for a sample of
      uids so this comment cannot quietly become false.

      The literal preset list is spelled out here rather than referenced,
      because a migration must keep meaning what it meant on the day it ran
      even after the Java list grows. That is also why growing PRESETS later is
      safe: rows written by this changeset keep the face they were given, and
      only profiles created afterwards see the wider list.

      Rows that already have an uploaded photo (avatar_resource_id is not null)
      are skipped — a photo wins over a preset everywhere a DTO is built
      (AvatarPresetService.presetFor), so a preset on such a row would be dead
      data that surfaces the day the photo is removed.

      Idempotent by its own WHERE clause: re-running writes nothing, and a
      profile row re-created later gets the same face from defaultFor.
    -->

    <changeSet id="2026-09-10-avatar-preset-backfill" author="mrodek">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <columnExists tableName="user_profiles" columnName="avatar_preset"/>
        </preConditions>

        <sql dbms="postgresql">
            update user_profiles
            set avatar_preset = (array[
                'kralj','baba','decko','dida','baka','gazda','konobar','cura',
                'momak','kibic','gospon','sudac','teta','profa','mornar','seka'
            ])[(('x' || substr(md5(user_uid), 1, 7))::bit(28)::bigint % 16) + 1]
            where avatar_preset is null
              and avatar_resource_id is null;
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <!-- Only undoes what this changeset did: rows still carrying the
                 face it computed, and no photo. A preset the user chose later
                 is left alone. -->
            <sql dbms="postgresql">
                update user_profiles
                set avatar_preset = null
                where avatar_resource_id is null
                  and avatar_preset = (array[
                      'kralj','baba','decko','dida','baka','gazda','konobar','cura',
                      'momak','kibic','gospon','sudac','teta','profa','mornar','seka'
                  ])[(('x' || substr(md5(user_uid), 1, 7))::bit(28)::bigint % 16) + 1];
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
